---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { PHASES } from '../data/briefings';
---

<Base
  title="Kevin Moore — Founder, Jochanni Labs — DAL-X"
  description="Kevin Moore is the founder of Jochanni Labs and author of the Decision Governance Strategic Intelligence Series — twenty briefings defining Decision Governance as the runtime authority discipline for AI-influenced decision paths."
>

  <!-- ── Page Header ──────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="km-header">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="km-header-inner">

        <div class="km-header-content">
          <p class="km-header-label">Author &nbsp;·&nbsp; Researcher &nbsp;·&nbsp; Jochanni Labs</p>
          <h1 class="km-header-name">Kevin Moore</h1>
          <div class="km-header-roles">
            <div class="km-role-row">
              <span class="km-role-title">Founder</span>
              <span class="km-role-org">Jochanni Labs</span>
            </div>
            <div class="km-role-row">
              <span class="km-role-title">Author</span>
              <span class="km-role-org">Decision Governance Strategic Intelligence Series</span>
            </div>
            <div class="km-role-row">
              <span class="km-role-title">Category</span>
              <span class="km-role-org">Decision Governance · Runtime Authority</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="km-focus-card">
          <p class="km-focus-card-label">Research Focus</p>
          <ul class="km-focus-list">
            <li>Runtime Authority</li>
            <li>Decision Governance</li>
            <li>AI Execution Governance</li>
            <li>Trigger Logic</li>
            <li>Authority Routing</li>
            <li>Execution Gating</li>
            <li>Enterprise Governance</li>
            <li>DAL-X Category Formation</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <main>

    <!-- ── Section 01 — The Work ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="km-section">
      <div class="container">

        <div class="km-section-head">
          <p class="km-section-marker">01 &nbsp;·&nbsp; The Work</p>
          <h2 class="km-section-title">What This Work Is</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="km-prose-grid">
          <div class="km-prose-col">
            <p>
              Kevin Moore's work is the definitional development of Decision Governance
              as an organizational discipline — the runtime authority layer that governs
              AI-influenced decisions before they produce consequence. The work is not
              commentary on AI. It is not technology strategy or deployment advice.
              It is the structural definition of a discipline that does not yet have a
              name in most enterprises.
            </p>
            <p>
              The question the work is built to answer is specific: when AI participates
              in a consequential decision path, who says that path is authorized to move
              forward? That question has no satisfactory answer inside most enterprise
              AI governance frameworks today. The frameworks that exist were built to
              manage AI systems — to inventory them, classify their risk, and document
              applicable policies. None of that work answers the authority question at
              the moment it matters, which is before consequence occurs.
            </p>
            <p>
              Decision Governance closes that structural gap. Moore is developing the
              framework — its vocabulary, its architectural requirements, its operational
              mechanics — through Jochanni Labs and the Decision Governance Strategic Intelligence
              Series. The work is being done in publication form so that any organization
              that needs it can engage with it directly.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="km-prose-col">
            <p>
              The work covers the specific operational requirements that runtime authority
              demands: trigger logic that converts AI participation into a governance
              event, authority routing that maps the decision path to the correct principal,
              execution gating that enforces scope, conformance, and acknowledgment
              simultaneously before action, decision recording that captures the authority
              record before consequence, and audit evidence that proves governance was
              active — not retrospective.
            </p>
            <p>
              These are not theoretical requirements. They are the operational gaps that
              exist inside every enterprise AI deployment that lacks a runtime authority
              layer. The sectors with the highest AI deployment rates — financial services,
              healthcare, legal operations, insurance, government — are also the sectors
              with the clearest legal and institutional consequences when ungoverned
              AI-influenced work produces harm, error, or challenge.
            </p>
            <p>
              The work is being done now because the moment for defining the category is
              before regulatory pressure, organizational incident, or competitive
              disadvantage forces the issue. Categories defined in crisis are defined
              poorly. This work is an attempt to define this one correctly.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 02 — The Research Thesis ─────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="km-section km-section--raised">
      <div class="container">

        <div class="km-section-head">
          <p class="km-section-marker">02 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Research Thesis</p>
          <h2 class="km-section-title">The Execution Gap</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="km-thesis-block">
          <p class="km-thesis-text">
            The Execution Gap is the structural void that exists when enterprises deploy
            AI systems without building the authority layer that governs what those
            systems are permitted to move toward action. It is not a technology failure.
            It is an organizational design failure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="km-prose-wide">
          <p>
            Every consequential organizational decision carries implicit authority
            requirements. These requirements exist in policy, in control frameworks,
            in approval hierarchies, in escalation protocols. They exist because
            organizations have learned, at cost, that consequential work cannot depend
            on informal confidence. The same lesson has not been applied to
            AI-influenced decisions — not because the lesson is wrong, but because
            the governance infrastructure required to apply it at machine speed does
            not yet exist in most enterprises.
          </p>
          <p>
            The Execution Gap is the name Moore gave this void. AI systems produce
            output. Organizations act on that output. Between production and action,
            no active governing structure determines whether the decision path had
            standing to proceed. Post-execution audit can explain what happened.
            It cannot retroactively authorize what already moved forward.
          </p>
          <p>
            Closing the Execution Gap requires building the authority infrastructure
            before AI-influenced work reaches the execution point — not documenting
            policies that apply in theory, but creating the operational layer that
            enforces authority requirements in practice. That is what Decision
            Governance addresses, and what DAL-X is the emerging framework for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="km-gap-definition">
          <div class="km-gap-def-item">
            <p class="km-gap-def-label">What the gap is not</p>
            <p class="km-gap-def-text">
              A technology failure. A model performance problem. A risk classification
              gap. A policy documentation shortage. These are adjacent problems.
              They are not the Execution Gap.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="km-gap-def-divider"></div>
          <div class="km-gap-def-item">
            <p class="km-gap-def-label">What the gap is</p>
            <p class="km-gap-def-text">
              The absence of a runtime authority structure at the point where
              AI-influenced work would move toward consequence. The organizational
              design failure that governs it once rather than governing it at execution.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 03 — Decision Governance ──────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="km-section">
      <div class="container">

        <div class="km-section-head">
          <p class="km-section-marker">03 &nbsp;·&nbsp; The Discipline</p>
          <h2 class="km-section-title">Decision Governance</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="km-two-col">
          <div>
            <p class="km-prose">
              Decision Governance is the discipline of establishing, maintaining, and
              enforcing authority over AI-influenced decisions before they produce
              organizational consequence. Moore's work defines it as a distinct
              organizational category — separate from model governance, risk management,
              compliance preparation, and AI ethics.
            </p>
            <p class="km-prose">
              The distinction is structural. Model governance addresses the AI system.
              Risk management addresses the threat landscape. Compliance preparation
              addresses regulatory alignment. Decision Governance addresses the authority
              question at the execution point: was the decision path permitted to move
              forward, under whose authority, with what evidence, and with what record
              that proves governance was active before consequence occurred.
            </p>
            <p class="km-prose">
              Moore frames Decision Governance around three concurrent operational
              requirements. Scope: the AI-influenced decision path operated within its
              authorized jurisdictional boundaries. Conformance: the decision path meets
              the governance framework currently in effect. Acknowledgment: an accountable
              principal with correct authority has formally accepted responsibility before
              execution moved forward. All three must be active simultaneously. Two of
              three is not partial governance — it is broken governance that produces the
              appearance of control without its substance.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="km-discipline-card">
            <p class="km-discipline-card-label">Decision Governance</p>
            <p class="km-discipline-card-def">
              The discipline of establishing, maintaining, and enforcing authority
              over AI-influenced decisions before they produce organizational consequence.
            </p>
            <div class="km-discipline-sep"></div>
            <p class="km-discipline-attr-label">Distinguished From</p>
            <ul class="km-discipline-list">
              <li>
                <span class="km-discipline-item-name">Model Governance</span>
                <span class="km-discipline-item-note">Governs the AI system, not the decision path</span>
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="km-discipline-item-name">Risk Management</span>
                <span class="km-discipline-item-note">Addresses threat landscape, not execution authority</span>
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="km-discipline-item-name">Compliance Preparation</span>
                <span class="km-discipline-item-note">Aligns to regulation, does not enforce runtime authority</span>
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="km-discipline-item-name">AI Ethics</span>
                <span class="km-discipline-item-note">Addresses values, not operational authority enforcement</span>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 04 — The Series ───────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="km-section km-section--raised">
      <div class="container">

        <div class="km-section-head">
          <p class="km-section-marker">04 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Publication</p>
          <h2 class="km-section-title">Decision Governance Strategic Intelligence Series</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="km-prose-wide">
          <p>
            The Decision Governance Strategic Intelligence Series is the primary publication vehicle
            through which Moore is developing and releasing the Decision Governance
            framework. Twenty briefings, organized across four phases, building the
            complete framework from first principles through deployment reality and
            category defense. Each briefing is a substantive piece of framework
            development — not commentary, not opinion, not news.
          </p>
          <p>
            The Series is published by Jochanni Labs. It is open access — available
            to any organization that needs it. The decision to publish the framework
            openly is itself an architectural one: category definition requires that
            the vocabulary, the concepts, and the operational requirements be available
            before the market attempts to fill the category with insufficient substitutes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="km-series-stats">
          <div class="km-series-stat">
            <p class="km-series-stat-num">20</p>
            <p class="km-series-stat-label">Briefings</p>
          </div>
          <div class="km-series-stat-divider"></div>
          <div class="km-series-stat">
            <p class="km-series-stat-num">4</p>
            <p class="km-series-stat-label">Phases</p>
          </div>
          <div class="km-series-stat-divider"></div>
          <div class="km-series-stat">
            <p class="km-series-stat-num">~80k</p>
            <p class="km-series-stat-label">Words</p>
          </div>
          <div class="km-series-stat-divider"></div>
          <div class="km-series-stat">
            <p class="km-series-stat-num">1</p>
            <p class="km-series-stat-label">Discipline Defined</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="km-phase-grid">
          {PHASES.map(phase => (
            <div class="km-phase-card">
              <p class="km-phase-num">Phase {phase.number}</p>
              <p class="km-phase-name">{phase.name}</p>
              <p class="km-phase-range">{phase.range}</p>
              <p class="km-phase-summary">{phase.summary}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <div class="km-section-cta">
          <a href="/briefings" class="btn-primary">Read the Series</a>
          <a href="/dal-x" class="btn-outline">DAL-X Framework</a>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 05 — Jochanni Labs ────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="km-section">
      <div class="container">

        <div class="km-section-head">
          <p class="km-section-marker">05 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Institution</p>
          <h2 class="km-section-title">Jochanni Labs</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="km-two-col">
          <div>
            <p class="km-prose">
              Moore founded Jochanni Labs as the research institution through which the
              conceptual and operational foundation of Decision Governance is developed.
              The Labs is not a consultancy. It is not a product company. It is the
              research organization that holds and publishes the definitional work for
              the runtime authority discipline.
            </p>
            <p class="km-prose">
              The institutional form of Jochanni Labs — a research lab rather than a
              vendor, a consultancy, or a startup — is deliberate. Category-defining
              work requires intellectual credibility and independence. The work needs
              to be seen as framework development, not as sales infrastructure for
              a product that happens to use the same vocabulary.
            </p>
            <p class="km-prose">
              The Decision Governance Strategic Intelligence Series is the primary output of Jochanni
              Labs. Additional research, framework extensions, and sector-specific
              applications of Decision Governance will be published through the same
              institutional channel as the discipline develops.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="km-labs-card">
            <p class="km-labs-name">Jochanni Labs</p>
            <div class="km-labs-lines">
              <div class="km-labs-line">
                <span class="km-labs-line-label">Type</span>
                <span class="km-labs-line-val">Research institution</span>
              </div>
              <div class="km-labs-line">
                <span class="km-labs-line-label">Focus</span>
                <span class="km-labs-line-val">Decision Governance · Runtime Authority</span>
              </div>
              <div class="km-labs-line">
                <span class="km-labs-line-label">Output</span>
                <span class="km-labs-line-val">Decision Governance Strategic Intelligence Series</span>
              </div>
              <div class="km-labs-line">
                <span class="km-labs-line-label">Founder</span>
                <span class="km-labs-line-val">Kevin Moore</span>
              </div>
              <div class="km-labs-line">
                <span class="km-labs-line-label">Access</span>
                <span class="km-labs-line-val">Open · No registration required</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <a href="/jochanni-labs" class="km-labs-link">About Jochanni Labs →</a>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 06 — Category Formation ───────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="km-section km-section--raised">
      <div class="container">

        <div class="km-section-head">
          <p class="km-section-marker">06 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Category Formation</p>
          <h2 class="km-section-title">Why This Work Is Being Done Now</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="km-prose-wide">
          <p>
            Categories in organizational management and technology governance are
            typically defined in one of two ways: ahead of necessity, through
            deliberate intellectual development, or in reaction to crisis, when
            the absence of a category has produced consequences severe enough to
            force naming. The former produces better categories. The latter produces
            categories shaped by the incidents that forced them into existence —
            which means the vocabulary reflects the failure mode rather than the
            structural requirement.
          </p>
          <p>
            Decision Governance is being defined ahead of the crisis that would
            otherwise define it. The incidents that will force enterprises to
            confront the Execution Gap have not yet occurred at scale — though
            the structural conditions for them are being created by every AI
            system deployed without a runtime authority layer. When those incidents
            occur, the organizations that have engaged with Decision Governance as
            a framework will be better positioned to respond. The organizations
            that have not will be defining governance in the aftermath.
          </p>
          <p>
            Moore's work is positioned explicitly as category-originating research.
            The Decision Governance Strategic Intelligence Series is not responding to an existing
            category — it is building one. The vocabulary, the operational requirements,
            the architectural concepts, and the institutional disciplines that the
            Series defines are intended to be the reference framework for Decision
            Governance as the discipline develops and as the organizations that need
            it begin building the infrastructure it requires.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="km-position-statement">
          <p class="km-position-text">
            "The organizations that engage with this framework now are building the
            governance foundation that the next phase of AI-assisted enterprise
            operations will require. Runtime authority is not optional infrastructure.
            It is the layer that makes AI-assisted execution governable."
          </p>
          <p class="km-position-attr">
            — Decision Governance Strategic Intelligence Series, Briefing 07
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Footer CTA ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="km-footer-cta">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="km-footer-cta-label">Decision Governance Strategic Intelligence Series</p>
        <p class="km-footer-cta-text">
          Twenty briefings defining the runtime authority discipline for AI-assisted
          enterprise operations — published by Jochanni Labs.
        </p>
        <div class="btn-row">
          <a href="/briefings" class="btn-primary">Read All 20 Briefings</a>
          <a href="/decision-governance" class="btn-outline">Decision Governance</a>
          <a href="/dal-x" class="btn-outline">DAL-X Framework</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </main>
</Base>
